        <div class="slide-content">
            <h2><i class="fas fa-door-open"></i> Open/Closed Principle</h2>
            <p>Classes should be open for extension, but closed for modification. Use inheritance or interfaces to add new features without changing existing code.</p>
            <pre class="code-example"><code class="language-csharp">// BAD: Modifies class for every new enemy type
public class Enemy : MonoBehaviour {
    public void Attack() {
        // ...
    }
}
// GOOD: Extend with subclasses
public abstract class Enemy : MonoBehaviour {
    public abstract void Attack();
}
public class Zombie : Enemy {
    public override void Attack() { /* ... */ }
}
public class Robot : Enemy {
    public override void Attack() { /* ... */ }
}</code></pre>
            <p>Imagine a Calculator class with a calculate method. If you initially coded it to handle only addition and subtraction, adding multiplication and division would require modifying the existing calculate method. This violates the OCP</p>
            <p>A better approach would be to create an interface IOperation with a calculate method. Then, create separate classes like Addition, Subtraction, Multiplication, and Division, each implementing the IOperation interface. The Calculator class would then take an IOperation object as an argument, allowing you to add new operations by simply creating new implementing classes without modifying the original Calculator class</p>
            <pre class="code-example"><code class="language-csharp">// BAD: Calculator class violates OCP
public class Calculator
{
    public double Calculate(double a, double b, string operation)
    {
        if (operation == "add")
        {
            return a + b;
        }
        else if (operation == "subtract")
        {
            return a - b;
        }
        // To add new operations like multiply or divide,
        // this method would need to be modified.
        return 0;
    }
}

// GOOD: Calculator adheres to OCP using interfaces
public interface IOperation
{
    double Calculate(double a, double b);
}

public class Addition : IOperation
{
    public double Calculate(double a, double b) => a + b;
}

public class Subtraction : IOperation
{
    public double Calculate(double a, double b) => a - b;
}

public class Multiplication : IOperation
{
    public double Calculate(double a, double b) => a * b;
}

public class Division : IOperation
{
    public double Calculate(double a, double b) => a / b;
}

public class BetterCalculator
{
    public double Calculate(IOperation operation, double a, double b)
    {
        return operation.Calculate(a, b);
    }
}

// Usage Example:
// var calculator = new BetterCalculator();
// double resultAdd = calculator.Calculate(new Addition(), 10, 5); // 15
// double resultMultiply = calculator.Calculate(new Multiplication(), 10, 5); // 50
// New operations can be added without changing BetterCalculator.
</code></pre>
