        <div class="slide-content">
            <h2><i class="fas fa-exchange-alt"></i> Liskov Substitution Principle</h2>
            <p>Subclasses should be usable anywhere their parent class is expected. In Unity, this means using base types and interfaces correctly.</p>
            <pre class="code-example"><code class="language-csharp">public class PowerUp : MonoBehaviour {
    public virtual void Apply(Player player) { /* ... */ }
}
public class SpeedBoost : PowerUp {
    public override void Apply(Player player) { player.speed += 5; }
}
public class Shield : PowerUp {
    public override void Apply(Player player) { player.shielded = true; }
}
// Usage:
void GivePowerUp(PowerUp powerUp, Player player) {
    powerUp.Apply(player); // Works for any PowerUp subclass
}</code></pre>
            <p><strong>Common LSP Violation: Rectangle and Square</strong></p>
            <p>Consider a <code>Rectangle</code> base class and a <code>Square</code> subclass. Mathematically, a square is a type of rectangle. However, if the <code>Rectangle</code> class has methods to set width and height independently, a <code>Square</code> subclass that maintains equal sides will behave unexpectedly when its width or height is changed independently. This violates LSP because a <code>Square</code> cannot be substituted for a <code>Rectangle</code> without altering the inherent properties and potentially breaking client code that expects independent width and height manipulation.</p>
            <pre class="code-example"><code class="language-csharp">// BAD: Square violates LSP if Rectangle's methods allow independent width/height
public class Rectangle
{
    public virtual int Width { get; set; }
    public virtual int Height { get; set; }

    public int Area => Width * Height;
}

public class Square : Rectangle
{
    public override int Width
    {
        get => base.Width;
        set { base.Width = value; base.Height = value; }
    }

    public override int Height
    {
        get => base.Height;
        set { base.Width = value; base.Height = value; }
    }
}

// Client code (any part of the application that uses these shape classes) that expects Rectangle behavior might break:
// Rectangle rect = new Square();
// rect.Width = 5;
// rect.Height = 10; // For a Square, this would make Width also 10, breaking expectation

// GOOD: Design with LSP in mind, perhaps avoiding direct inheritance for Square
// or ensuring contracts are upheld. One solution is to use an interface:
public interface IShape
{
    int GetArea();
}

public class Rect : IShape
{
    public int Width { get; set; }
    public int Height { get; set; }
    public int GetArea() => Width * Height;
}

public class Sq : IShape
{
    public int Side { get; set; }
    public int GetArea() => Side * Side;
}

// Now, client code (any part of the application that uses these shapes) treats them as distinct shapes with area calculation,
// instead of relying on mutable dimensions that behave differently:
// IShape squareShape = new Sq { Side = 5 };
// IShape rectangleShape = new Rect { Width = 5, Height = 10 };
// Console.WriteLine(squareShape.GetArea()); // 25
// Console.WriteLine(rectangleShape.GetArea()); // 50
</code></pre>
